@TGT
Feature: a target whose fitness checks are converted scenarios

  Planted for @SCN-STG-005's @integration arm and used TWICE — this tree is byte-identical in
  the scn-tags-converted-unconfigured fixture beside it, and only the package.json differs.

  Both scenarios below carry the fitness axis tag and their @SCN and nothing else, which is
  the whole of what a converted check's scenario says about itself: the tag names the axis and
  the CHARACTERISTIC is on the configured row. Neither names a tier, and neither carries a word
  this target's registry names — so both red the shipped command as tags that mean nothing here
  unless the axis word is grammar in its own right.

  THE PAIR IS THE RULING RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT OF THE PLANT. SCN-TGT-001 is named by a
  configured row and SCN-TGT-002 is named by none — a half-converted check, whose tag moved and
  whose row was never written. Both are admitted. That second one IS a corpus fault, and it
  belongs to @SCN-LNT-020: it names the scenario by id, and the run reds on it regardless
  through the degenerate both-missing item. Redding it here too would put half of one fault in a
  second place with the wrong remedy attached — "this word means nothing here", said to an
  author whose scenario names the one axis the model has.

  AND THE SISTER TREE CONFIGURES NO ROWS AT ALL, where both scenarios still pass. Which words
  name a CHECK is the target's answer; which axis a scenario is ON is the model's, exactly as
  `unit` is — so the axis word is not the target's to grant or to withhold, and a grammar
  gating it on the manifest would red here while the obligation rule — which reads the word with
  no such gate — was naming SCN-TGT-001 and SCN-TGT-002 as scenarios on that very axis.

  @fitness @SCN-TGT-001
  Scenario: the codebase stays inside the complexity thresholds

  @fitness @SCN-TGT-002
  Scenario: a converted scenario no configured row names
